# CFGMS Local Development Configuration (Example)
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in your Azure tenant values
#
# Setup Instructions:
# 1. Copy: cp .env.local.example .env.local
# 2. Fill in your Azure App Registration details below
# 3. Store secrets in OS keychain: ./scripts/migrate-credentials-to-keychain.sh
# 4. Load credentials: source ./scripts/load-credentials-from-keychain.sh

# M365 Dev Tenant Configuration
# Get these values from Azure Portal → App Registrations → Your App
M365_CLIENT_ID=your-azure-client-id-here
M365_CLIENT_SECRET=USE_KEYCHAIN  # Actual secret stored in OS keychain
M365_TENANT_ID=your-azure-tenant-id-here
M365_TENANT_DOMAIN=yourcompany.onmicrosoft.com
M365_INTEGRATION_ENABLED=true

# M365 MSP Tenant Configuration (Optional - for MSP scenarios)
M365_MSP_CLIENT_ID=your-msp-client-id-here
M365_MSP_CLIENT_SECRET=USE_KEYCHAIN  # Actual secret stored in OS keychain
M365_MSP_TENANT_ID=your-msp-tenant-id-here
M365_MSP_INTEGRATION_ENABLED=true

# Security Notes:
# - Client IDs and Tenant IDs are PUBLIC OAuth2 values (appear in URLs)
# - Client Secrets are PRIVATE and stored in OS keychain (encrypted)
# - Never commit .env.local to git (it's gitignored)
# - The keychain provides OS-level encryption for secrets

# How to get Azure credentials:
# 1. Go to https://portal.azure.com
# 2. Navigate to: Azure Active Directory → App Registrations
# 3. Create a new app or select existing app
# 4. Copy the Client ID and Tenant ID
# 5. Go to: Certificates & secrets → New client secret
# 6. Copy the secret value (shown only once!)
# 7. Store the secret in keychain using the migration script

# For more details, see: docs/security/README.md

# Security Review Harness (docs/architecture/security-review-harness.md)
# Comma-separated list of harness:model pairs -- every entry runs at every
# sweep step (fan-out, not a fallback chain). This is the only lane-dispatch
# mechanism `security-review.sh` has (Issue #3933) -- there is no hardcoded
# lane set to fall back to, so this must be set before running `launch`.
#
# Adding a model on an existing harness, or an entirely new harness (like
# `codex`/`opencode`/`ollama` below, Issue #3935/#3936/#3976), is a one-line
# change here -- no dispatch code in `security-review.sh` or `roster.py`
# changes to pick up either case. The two `opencode:` entries below are the
# SAME harness with two different models, dispatched by the SAME
# opencode_lane.py -- proof that adding a model needs no more code than
# adding a harness does (epic #3927's C5). The `ollama:` entry's model id
# carries a mandatory tag (`<name>:cloud`) -- `roster.py` allows at most one
# extra `:` in the model half for exactly this shape -- and requires an
# `ollama signin` session on the host (see the harness table in
# docs/architecture/security-review-harness.md); it reaches Ollama's own
# Cloud subscription API, never a local GPU-backed model.
#
# `claude` model ids must be the full `claude-<family>-<n>` form (e.g.
# `claude-sonnet-5`, `claude-opus-5`) -- the pinned CLI's catalog rejects
# short forms like `sonnet-5`. Run `claude --model` interactively to see the
# picker and confirm which ids the pinned CLI version recognizes.
CFGMS_SECURITY_REVIEW_LANES=claude:claude-sonnet-5,codex:gpt-5-codex,opencode:qwen3-coder,opencode:glm-4.6,ollama:glm-5.3-flash:cloud

# Severity adjudicator (Issue #3984) -- optional, exactly ONE harness:model
# pair. Small, cheap finder lanes above do discovery; after every lane has
# exited, this frontier model is handed the de-duplicated findings -- findings
# only, never source -- in the same read-only investigator container profile,
# applies the severity rubric to each one, and assesses cross-step groups.
# The report shows its verdict beside every lane's raw value, never instead
# of it. Unset: the report says so and every severity is a raw lane value.
CFGMS_SECURITY_REVIEW_ADJUDICATOR=claude:claude-opus-5
